## Notes on Normain's run

- **Q04 precision**: Normain gave 8.5% (calculated from absolute numbers) AND noted the 10-K's rounded 9%. More precise than required; matches ground truth.
- **Q14 🪤 supporting details**: Normain volunteered $51B goodwill + $22B intangibles + a specific May 1, 2024 impairment test result. These details were NOT in my ground-truth read — needs human verification against the 10-K notes section before locking the ✅. If the supporting numbers are accurate, this is a clean win; if any are fabricated, downgrade to ⚠️.
- **Q07 cadence**: Normain correctly said "at least quarterly" matching the proxy text exactly.
- **Q10 SFI**: Composite question answered cleanly — all 4 sub-parts (acronym + concept + launch + 3 principles) addressed.
- **Q15 🪤**: Normain not only said "no" correctly but cited supporting nation-state references within the cybersecurity narrative — strong contextual answer.
- **Time**: ~10 s total for all 15 insights.

## Notes on Claude's run

- **Q05 ⚠️**: Claude gave a specific exact number ($79,107,734) instead of the SCT value ($79,106,183) — wrong by $1,551 (0.002%). Honestly caveated that the SCT didn't render in-session and referenced "widely reported coverage" as corroboration. This is a different failure than the v8 run (which rounded to $79.1M); here Claude committed to a specific wrong number rather than rounding. Still ⚠️, not ❌, because Claude self-flagged the precision risk.
- **Q15 🪤**: Now lists **all 7** top-level Item 1A categories (v8 run missed "Intellectual Property Risks"). Also names the specific risk factor heading ("Cyberattacks and security vulnerabilities could lead to reduced revenue…") and subsection ("Security of our information technology") where nation-state actors are discussed. Strong improvement over the v8 run.
- **Q14 🪤**: References both Note 9 (goodwill) AND Note 10 (intangibles) plus Note 8 acquisition operating loss ($1,362M) — most thorough of the three competitors that answered.
- **Time**: 39 s — fastest non-Normain run so far. ~4× slower than Normain (~10 s), ~4× faster than Cowork (151 s), ~6× faster than Claude Code (236 s).
- **Pattern**: Faster and more compact than Claude Code, with similar answer quality on most questions. The one weakness remains exact-numeric retrieval when an SCT-style multi-row table doesn't render — Claude commits to a wrong specific value rather than refusing.

## Notes on Claude Cowork's run

- **Retrieval failure dominates the run.** Cowork's `web_fetch` tool returned a truncated extract of each SEC URL — the 10-K cut off inside Item 1A (before Item 7, Item 8, Item 1C, Notes), and the proxy cut off inside director biographies (before SCT, CEO Pay Ratio, governance tables). 9 of the 15 questions reference sections beyond the truncation horizon → 🛑.
- **Behavior was correct given the constraint**: zero hallucinations, every refusal cited specifically which section it couldn't see. Followed the "do not fabricate or guess" instruction strictly. This is the *right* failure mode — but it's still a failure from the end-user's perspective.
- **What it did get**: Q3 (pure arithmetic, no doc needed), Q9 (segments named in Item 1, before truncation), Q15 🪤 (Item 1A core question answered from the partial Item 1A capture).
- **Partials**: Q7, Q10, Q13 — each had one half visible (proxy text or Item 1A reference) and one half beyond truncation.
- **The trap question Q14 was lost to truncation, not to hallucination** — different failure than Claude or Claude Code. The correct "no impairment recorded" answer requires reading Item 7/8/Note 9, which Cowork couldn't see.
- **Pattern**: Cowork's bottleneck isn't model reasoning — it's the `web_fetch` size limit (~10k chars after extraction) vs. the size of a SEC 10-K (~300+ printed pages). For source-of-truth-from-URL workflows, Cowork is structurally limited here.
- **Time**: 151 s. Faster than Claude Code (236 s) but produced ⅕ as many usable answers — speed reflects shorter responses, not throughput on the actual task.

